WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:03.090
Imagine a colleague who has been asked to lead the

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.770
monthly check-in with outside partners for the first time. Two

3
00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:09.890
days before, she tells her supervisor she is worried. She

4
00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.650
has never run a meeting like this, and one partner

5
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.750
is known for pushing back hard. The supervisor has twenty

6
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:16.890
minutes free that afternoon.

7
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.100
Twenty minutes is not much time, and the temptation to

8
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.680
just fix it for her must be strong. So what

9
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.520
is coaching, or developmental, leadership?

10
00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:30.750
It treats everyday challenges as chances to learn. The leader

11
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.850
partners with each person to build long term skill and

12
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.690
judgment, and to connect their career goals with the mission.

13
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.650
The core move is inquiry before instruction. When someone brings

14
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.310
a problem, the leader resists solving it for them, and

15
00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:44.830
asks good questions instead.

16
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.440
Does that mean a coaching leader avoids giving hard feedback,

17
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.440
and keeps everything positive and encouraging all the time?

18
00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.710
The opposite. The page is clear that a skilled coach

19
00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:59.270
does not hold back difficult feedback to be nice, because

20
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.270
withholding it takes away the chance to grow. The leader

21
00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.730
still holds accountability for performance and standards. They simply use

22
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.590
that authority mainly to help people develop.

23
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.840
What would I notice about a coaching leader in an

24
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.980
ordinary month? What makes their conversations feel different from a

25
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:14.740
regular check-in?

26
00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.390
Regular one to one meetings that go beyond status to

27
00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:23.070
skills, goals and lessons from setbacks. Specific, timely feedback tied

28
00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.010
to real examples, both what is working and what to

29
00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:29.050
grow. Stretch assignments, like leading a cross unit meeting or

30
00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:32.750
drafting a policy brief, with support to debrief afterward. Questions

31
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:33.470
before advice.

32
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.290
And real advocacy for where each person wants to be

33
00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.150
in a few years.

34
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.060
Where does this approach pay off the most? It sounds

35
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.700
like a long term investment in people rather than a

36
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.140
quick fix.

37
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:50.110
It is, and the returns compound. Coaching someone through a

38
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.870
task takes longer than doing it yourself, but once they

39
00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:57.350
master it, the team's capacity grows for good. The page

40
00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:00.610
notes that in public agencies facing retirements and tight hiring

41
00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.490
budgets, coaching is one of the most cost effective ways

42
00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.990
to grow leaders from within and keep valuable knowledge.

43
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.200
And the limits? I can think of moments when a

44
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.340
long coaching conversation is simply the wrong tool.

45
00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:14.610
In a crisis that needs a decision in ten minutes,

46
00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.390
coaching is the wrong tool. A supervisor with twenty or

47
00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.530
more direct reports and a crushing caseload cannot coach everyone

48
00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:25.190
deeply without structural support. And coaching cannot fix an unwillingness

49
00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:28.910
to meet basic job expectations, which calls for clear accountability.

50
00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.210
There's also an organizational risk.

51
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:35.790
When coaching is optional, some units thrive while others stagnate.

52
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.340
The page calls coaching one of the most powerful levers

53
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.680
for workplace equity, if it is applied fairly. What makes

54
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.380
it so powerful?

55
00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.250
Advancement often depends on unwritten rules, such as how to

56
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.650
frame a memo for executives or prepare for a promotional

57
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.790
interview. People without insider networks often never hear those rules.

58
00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.590
A good coach makes them explicit.

59
00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.810
Coaching also closes what researchers call the feedback gap, where

60
00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.410
some supervisors give vague or overly cautious feedback to colleagues

61
00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:09.250
of color, women or disabled staff, who are then blindsided

62
00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.430
when they are passed over.

63
00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.260
And the risks? I suspect much of it comes down

64
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.800
to who gets those twenty minutes, and who never does.

65
00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:22.270
Exactly. Busy managers ration their coaching, and without noticing they

66
00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.890
tend to invest it in people they naturally click with.

67
00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:29.070
That widens gaps. A second, subtler risk is coaching people

68
00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.850
to conform, for example pushing a quiet, analytical leader to

69
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.190
be louder, or asking a neurodivergent colleague to mask harmless

70
00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.650
differences, instead of focusing on actual results.

71
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.240
So back to those twenty minutes before the partner meeting.

72
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.020
Taking over the meeting would protect it, but it would

73
00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:45.920
not help her grow.

74
00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.450
Spend the time asking questions: what would a good meeting

75
00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:51.950
look like to her, what worries her most, and how

76
00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.910
might she respond if the partner pushes back. Then agree

77
00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:57.550
on one thing she will try, and a short conversation

78
00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.170
afterward. The plan is hers, and the meeting becomes learning,

79
00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.870
whatever happens in the room.

80
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.900
What does the page suggest so that coaching reaches everyone

81
00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.140
on a team, and not only the people a leader

82
00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.180
happens to click with?

83
00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:14.330
Make structured career conversations an expectation for every supervisor with

84
00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.410
every direct report, at least twice a year. Train coaches

85
00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:21.370
to separate real performance standards from personal style preferences. And

86
00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:24.690
track who receives coaching, stretch assignments and promotions across groups,

87
00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.310
to make sure development reaches everyone fairly.

88
00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.420
Here is a question to carry with you. Coaching time

89
00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:34.660
is a resource. Who on your team usually gets twenty

90
00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.300
minutes like this from you, and who does not? Thank

91
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:37.840
you for listening.
